(If it's 6°C...)

1289
00:48:14,762 --> 00:48:17,422
I probably won't need a jacket.

1290
00:48:18,762 --> 00:48:20,462
As fall has come,

1291
00:48:20,792 --> 00:48:22,702
I'm planning to go on a backpacking trip.

1292
00:48:23,102 --> 00:48:26,132
I had friends around me who enjoying backpacking,

1293
00:48:26,432 --> 00:48:28,501
so I followed them from time to time.

1294
00:48:28,502 --> 00:48:30,402
It's perfect to go out right now.

1295
00:48:30,742 --> 00:48:32,142
- It's perfect right now. - Right.

1296
00:48:32,812 --> 00:48:34,982
It's my second time going alone.

1297
00:48:37,012 --> 00:48:38,811
Isn't that the rice she won last time?

1298
00:48:38,812 --> 00:48:39,952
- Right. - Right.

1299
00:48:40,352 --> 00:48:42,822
Gosh, it feels nice to see it.

1300
00:48:45,022 --> 00:48:47,068
(She puts in the minced garlic into an empty container.)

1301
00:48:47,092 --> 00:48:48,492
She must be planning to cook.

1302
00:48:48,822 --> 00:48:50,392
She has to make food there.

1303
00:48:53,592 --> 00:48:55,962
(She packs just what she needs with the ingredients.)

1304
00:48:58,302 --> 00:48:59,831
She must go camping often.

1305
00:48:59,832 --> 00:49:01,432
She has all of those containers as well.

1306
00:49:01,642 --> 00:49:04,742
I think they're containers for cosmetics. I just used them.

1307
00:49:04,912 --> 00:49:07,392
It's most important to cut down on the weight of the backpack.

1308
00:49:09,712 --> 00:49:11,352
(Cutting)

1309
00:49:14,082 --> 00:49:16,762
(The salt is packed with foil. Liquids are packed into containers.)

1310
00:49:18,152 --> 00:49:19,292
How diligent.

1311
00:49:19,322 --> 00:49:21,962
She seems like an expert by the way she's packing things.

1312
00:49:22,792 --> 00:49:25,592
(She prepares a cooler bag for the ingredients.)

1313
00:49:25,932 --> 00:49:27,261
You end up buying everything for a camping trip...

1314
00:49:27,262 --> 00:49:28,601
if you don't go regularly.

1315
00:49:28,602 --> 00:49:31,301
- I'm a camper who buys everything. - A fake camper.

1316
00:49:31,302 --> 00:49:32,401
- I see. - Right.

1317
00:49:32,402 --> 00:49:33,931
I lend my friends with things.

1318
00:49:33,932 --> 00:49:35,472
This is you when you go camping.

1319
00:49:35,502 --> 00:49:37,241
You spend more money than going to a five-star hotel.

1320
00:49:37,242 --> 00:49:38,441
- I see. - Gosh.

1321
00:49:38,442 --> 00:49:40,011
I've been quite distant from camping these days.

1322
00:49:40,012 --> 00:49:41,271
Do you still have that car?

1323
00:49:41,272 --> 00:49:42,941
- I sold it. - He sold it.

1324
00:49:42,942 --> 00:49:46,052
Hyun Moo is doing a great job with making Korea's economy flow.

1325
00:49:46,152 --> 00:49:47,412
(Feeling proud)

1326
00:49:48,152 --> 00:49:49,852
(She packs her backpack diligently.)

1327
00:49:51,022 --> 00:49:52,851
That backpack can carry so many things.

1328
00:49:52,852 --> 00:49:54,591
- Right. - How many liters can it carry?

1329
00:49:54,592 --> 00:49:55,662
It can carry 70L.

1330
00:49:55,962 --> 00:49:56,962
Did you say 70L?

1331
00:49:57,562 --> 00:49:58,932
That's the extra-large one.

1332
00:49:59,292 --> 00:50:00,562
(Putting in)

1333
00:50:02,562 --> 00:50:04,162
(Struggling)

1334
00:50:04,872 --> 00:50:07,302
(It's becoming harder to put things in.)

1335
00:50:08,742 --> 00:50:10,442
The light things go in first.

1336
00:50:10,642 --> 00:50:12,848
The heavy things are placed closer toward the back as possible.

1337
00:50:12,872 --> 00:50:14,741
I try to place the heavy things...

1338
00:50:14,742 --> 00:50:16,641
- toward the upper back. - Since the heavy things...

1339
00:50:16,642 --> 00:50:17,982
might make you lean back.

1340
00:50:18,382 --> 00:50:19,911
It's less harder...

1341
00:50:19,912 --> 00:50:22,751
if the heavy things are placed against your back.

1342
00:50:22,752 --> 00:50:25,232
- Seoul National University! - Otherwise, you'd fall backward.

1343
00:50:25,792 --> 00:50:27,621
I think it's right to place the heavy things on top.

1344
00:50:27,622 --> 00:50:28,762
- I guess so. - Right.

1345
00:50:31,222 --> 00:50:34,031
(She even uses the sides of the backpack.)

1346
00:50:34,032 --> 00:50:36,331
How heavy was that backpack then?

1347
00:50:36,332 --> 00:50:38,672
I think it was around 15 to 20kg.

1348
00:50:38,702 --> 00:50:40,602
- Just like for an army march. - Right.

1349
00:50:43,872 --> 00:50:44,942
Heave-ho!

1350
00:50:49,142 --> 00:50:50,441
(Sliding down)

1351
00:50:50,442 --> 00:50:52,052
Gosh, I'm already exhausted.

1352
00:50:52,552 --> 00:50:55,121
Everyone rests like that during a march.

1353
00:50:55,122 --> 00:50:56,551
Right.

1354
00:50:56,552 --> 00:50:58,168
- Just like that. - Everyone is like that.

1355
00:50:58,192 --> 00:50:59,691
I'm seeing that pose after ten years.

1356
00:50:59,692 --> 00:51:00,751
Gosh.

1357
00:51:00,752 --> 00:51:03,061
The snack given is eaten just like that.

1358
00:51:03,062 --> 00:51:05,262
Right. Everyone sleeps on the concrete floor like that.

1359
00:51:05,662 --> 00:51:08,332
That's tough to carry even for men.

1360
00:51:08,362 --> 00:51:10,362
With rotational force,

1361
00:51:10,702 --> 00:51:11,871
it isn't that hard to carry.

1362
00:51:11,872 --> 00:51:12,931
Seoul National University!

1363
00:51:12,932 --> 00:51:14,472
(Gosh, rotational force?)

1364
00:51:17,572 --> 00:51:18,671
(Getting up)

1365
00:51:18,672 --> 00:51:20,041
Is that okay?

1366
00:51:20,042 --> 00:51:21,072
It must be heavy.

1367
00:51:21,982 --> 00:51:24,263
(She carries the backpack with the scientific principle.)

1368
00:51:24,642 --> 00:51:25,712
Let's go.

1369
00:51:26,882 --> 00:51:29,081
Gosh, let's go.

1370
00:51:29,082 --> 00:51:31,152
(She sets out for her march.)

1371
00:51:38,632 --> 00:51:39,792
The weather is great.

1372
00:51:42,432 --> 00:51:45,302
(She walks while humming.)

1373
00:51:46,672 --> 00:51:48,102
She must not be driving there.

1374
00:51:48,342 --> 00:51:50,202
Is there a camping site near her place?

1375
00:51:50,842 --> 00:51:52,372
The weather is really nice today.

1376
00:51:52,672 --> 00:51:53,842
It feels cool.

1377
00:51:55,812 --> 00:51:58,012
I hope the autumn leaves look beautiful.

1378
00:51:59,282 --> 00:52:00,882
(Rummaging)

1379
00:52:02,782 --> 00:52:03,882
Gosh, the 20th minute.

1380
00:52:05,352 --> 00:52:07,272
- What is it? - It will come on the 20th minute.

1381
00:52:08,992 --> 00:52:12,391
I purposely chose not to drive today.

1382
00:52:12,392 --> 00:52:15,032
It feels like an opportunity to experience more coincidences.

1383
00:52:15,132 --> 00:52:17,631
Also, when I get there when I'm all exhausted,

1384
00:52:17,632 --> 00:52:19,131
it feels a lot more rewarding.

1385
00:52:19,132 --> 00:52:20,932
It's like putting my body to the test.

1386
00:52:21,372 --> 00:52:22,902
Right. You need to feel that.

1387
00:52:23,602 --> 00:52:25,972
- There's someone who understands. - Definitely.

1388
00:52:26,842 --> 00:52:29,212
The campground I will be going to...

1389
00:52:29,382 --> 00:52:31,442
needs me to take the Gyeongui-Jungang Line...

1390
00:52:31,682 --> 00:52:33,612
and a town bus.

1391
00:52:34,182 --> 00:52:35,651
(It takes around two hours.)

1392
00:52:35,652 --> 00:52:39,151
The town bus goes around only 3 to 4 times a day.

1393
00:52:39,152 --> 00:52:40,951
- It'll be terrible if I miss it. - Gosh.

1394
00:52:40,952 --> 00:52:42,791
- What happens if you miss it? - I rushed in the morning...

1395
00:52:42,792 --> 00:52:44,392
to take that bus.

1396
00:52:44,722 --> 00:52:47,131
I have to wait for four hours if I miss the town bus.

1397
00:52:47,132 --> 00:52:48,231
That can't happen.

1398
00:52:48,232 --> 00:52:50,702
- You have to hurry then. - I had to be there on time.

1399
00:52:50,862 --> 00:52:51,871
The 20th minute.

1400
00:52:51,872 --> 00:52:52,902
(In a hurry)

1401
00:52:53,302 --> 00:52:55,141
The subway for the Gyeongui-Jungang Line...

1402
00:52:55,142 --> 00:52:57,041
- comes every 30 minutes. - Right. They don't come often.

1403
00:52:57,042 --> 00:52:59,042
You will miss the town bus if you miss the subway.

1404
00:52:59,142 --> 00:53:01,942
(I'll miss the town bus if I miss this subway!)

1405
00:53:02,842 --> 00:53:03,881
(Running)

1406
00:53:03,882 --> 00:53:04,982
The 20th minute!

1407
00:53:05,712 --> 00:53:07,581
It's coming in 4 minutes! There are 3 minutes left!

1408
00:53:07,582 --> 00:53:09,598
I don't think you can make it with four minutes left.

1409
00:53:09,622 --> 00:53:10,622
Gosh, she's quick.

1410
00:53:10,623 --> 00:53:12,152
- Gosh. - Gosh.

1411
00:53:13,152 --> 00:53:15,321
(Oksain Bolt gets summoned as she's in a hurry.)

1412
00:53:15,322 --> 00:53:16,462
Oksain Bolt.

1413
00:53:17,622 --> 00:53:19,461
I couldn't beat her at all.

1414
00:53:19,462 --> 00:53:21,531
How can she be beaten when she's training all the time...

1415
00:53:21,532 --> 00:53:22,832
on her own like this?

1416
00:53:22,962 --> 00:53:24,431
Toward Yangjeong Station.

1417
00:53:24,432 --> 00:53:25,472
(Panting)

1418
00:53:26,832 --> 00:53:28,542
(Panting)

1419
00:53:29,402 --> 00:53:30,472
Okay.

1420
00:53:30,772 --> 00:53:32,111
- She made it? - She still made it.

1421
00:53:32,112 --> 00:53:33,372
- That's amazing. - I made it.

1422
00:53:34,712 --> 00:53:37,942
Why does the view from the subway look so beautiful today?

1423
00:53:38,852 --> 00:53:39,911
(Donong Station)

1424
00:53:39,912 --> 00:53:41,581
(She takes the subway for around 40 minutes.)

1425
00:53:41,582 --> 00:53:42,582
(Asin Station)

1426
00:53:43,152 --> 00:53:44,221
(She arrives at Asin Station.)

1427
00:53:44,222 --> 00:53:45,752
- Asin? - Asin.

1428
00:53:47,722 --> 00:53:50,062
The air already feels better.

1429
00:53:54,162 --> 00:53:56,502
(She quickly shops for groceries.)

1430
00:53:57,602 --> 00:53:58,702
Here it is!

1431
00:54:00,532 --> 00:54:02,402
(Let's see.)

1432
00:54:02,802 --> 00:54:03,841
(Asin-ri, Asin Station Entrance)

1433
00:54:03,842 --> 00:54:06,072
- This is the bus stop? - Asin-ri, Asin Station.

1434
00:54:07,472 --> 00:54:09,112
(Heave-ho)

1435
00:54:10,612 --> 00:54:13,151
(Shall we take a breather?)

1436
00:54:13,152 --> 00:54:15,452
(But the bus comes right away.)

1437
00:54:16,822 --> 00:54:17,922
It's here.

1438
00:54:19,222 --> 00:54:21,821
(I must not miss it.)

1439
00:54:21,822 --> 00:54:23,792
(Run, Ja Yeon!)

1440
00:54:25,292 --> 00:54:26,432
Hello.

1441
00:54:27,532 --> 00:54:29,462
I'm headed to the recreational forest.

1442
00:54:32,832 --> 00:54:35,401
Why did you tell the driver where you're going?

1443
00:54:35,402 --> 00:54:36,502
Are you showing off?

1444
00:54:37,902 --> 00:54:39,941
"You know that that I'm Actress Ok Ja Yeon, right?"

1445
00:54:39,942 --> 00:54:41,342
"I'm not Ok Ja Yeon."

1446
00:54:41,812 --> 00:54:42,812
No.

1447
00:54:42,813 --> 00:54:45,351
The last stop of that bus is the end of town,

1448
00:54:45,352 --> 00:54:47,782
but the recreational forest is further up top from the town.

1449
00:54:47,952 --> 00:54:49,581
It'll be a one-hour walk from the last stop,

1450
00:54:49,582 --> 00:54:52,322
but the driver will take you there if you tell him.

1451
00:54:52,722 --> 00:54:53,722
(Amazed)

1452
00:54:53,723 --> 00:54:55,461
So the driver won't go up there if you don't tell him.

1453
00:54:55,462 --> 00:54:56,691
- That's really nice. - That's the charm...

1454
00:54:56,692 --> 00:54:58,992
- of buses in the countryside. - There's that charm.

1455
00:54:59,132 --> 00:55:01,161
Since a lot of elders live there,

1456
00:55:01,162 --> 00:55:02,601
the drivers take them to where they need to be.

1457
00:55:02,602 --> 00:55:04,901
Right. They can even say their addresses.

1458
00:55:04,902 --> 00:55:06,871
- Right. - Right.

1459
00:55:06,872 --> 00:55:09,541
- That's a good culture. - Right.

1460
00:55:09,542 --> 00:55:11,772
- Korean culture. - Yes. Good culture.

1461
00:55:14,312 --> 00:55:16,642
It's great as it isn't cold.

1462
00:55:17,642 --> 00:55:18,852
It feels so cool.

1463
00:55:21,922 --> 00:55:24,682
(She looks out the window...)

1464
00:55:26,252 --> 00:55:27,691
(and the friendly scenery can be seen.)

1465
00:55:27,692 --> 00:55:30,292
Bus rides are the best in the countryside.

1466
00:55:31,462 --> 00:55:33,132
(Looking around)

1467
00:55:35,462 --> 00:55:36,701
The view on the other side was better.

1468
00:55:36,702 --> 00:55:37,901
I see.

1469
00:55:37,902 --> 00:55:39,371
- I do that at times as well. - Right.

1470
00:55:39,372 --> 00:55:41,012
(She moved to a seat with a better view.)

1471
00:55:41,272 --> 00:55:43,242
(Focused)

1472
00:55:43,972 --> 00:55:46,642
The countryside houses look great. There are crocks?

1473
00:55:46,712 --> 00:55:47,942
Gosh, there are crocks.

1474
00:55:50,342 --> 00:55:52,081
You can't see those things when you are driving.

1475
00:55:52,082 --> 00:55:53,151
- You only see what's ahead of you. - Right.

1476
00:55:53,152 --> 00:55:54,552
I enjoy taking the bus as well.

1477
00:55:55,652 --> 00:55:58,222
(After enjoying the bus vibes for a while, )

1478
00:55:59,192 --> 00:56:00,951
(the bus goes up the curvy slope.)

1479
00:56:00,952 --> 00:56:03,021
Gosh, it's going really high up.

1480
00:56:03,022 --> 00:56:05,691
You almost walked for an hour if you didn't know that.

1481
00:56:05,692 --> 00:56:06,792
Right.

1482
00:56:08,532 --> 00:56:09,702
(Clapping)

1483
00:56:11,502 --> 00:56:13,371
(She gets there comfortably thanks to the driver.)

1484
00:56:13,372 --> 00:56:14,571
So there's a place like that.

1485
00:56:14,572 --> 00:56:15,871
(Recreational Forest)

1486
00:56:15,872 --> 00:56:18,272
- This place is very popular. - I see.

1487
00:56:18,372 --> 00:56:19,571
It's hard to book a spot.

1488
00:56:19,572 --> 00:56:20,771
- This place? - Yes.

1489
00:56:20,772 --> 00:56:22,181
- Why? - Everyone dreams of...

1490
00:56:22,182 --> 00:56:23,981
- going wild camping, - Right.

1491
00:56:23,982 --> 00:56:25,311
- but it's hard to actually do that. - Right.

1492
00:56:25,312 --> 00:56:27,551
It's a recreational forest where you can enjoy wild camping vibes.

1493
00:56:27,552 --> 00:56:29,298
(A place where you can enjoy wild camping vibes!)

1494
00:56:29,322 --> 00:56:31,791
- It's a place full of nature. - Right.

1495
00:56:31,792 --> 00:56:33,721
You have to book a spot in advance?

1496
00:56:33,722 --> 00:56:35,591
- Yes. - So you can't stay at...

1497
00:56:35,592 --> 00:56:37,161
any mountain you wish to stay at, right?

1498
00:56:37,162 --> 00:56:39,161
If you were to say, "I'm going to sleep on this mountain..."

1499
00:56:39,162 --> 00:56:40,362
That means you're just lost.

1500
00:56:41,262 --> 00:56:42,632
(Understanding)

1501
00:56:44,402 --> 00:56:46,101
(She arrives at her destination after around two hours.)

1502
00:56:46,102 --> 00:56:48,032
It's really far away.

1503
00:56:48,332 --> 00:56:50,042
So this is the spot?

1504
00:56:50,702 --> 00:56:52,342
- Hello. - Hello.

1505
00:56:53,012 --> 00:56:54,542
- Did you make a reservation? - Yes.

1506
00:56:55,012 --> 00:56:56,982
- I'm Ok Ja Yeon. - Right.

1507
00:56:57,212 --> 00:56:58,242
Right.

1508
00:56:58,852 --> 00:57:01,981
You may never make a fire with any firewood.

1509
00:57:01,982 --> 00:57:04,151
Firewood isn't allowed, but I can use a stove.

1510
00:57:04,152 --> 00:57:06,592
- Right. - You can make simple dishes.

1511
00:57:06,992 --> 00:57:08,921
- This is our current location. - Right.

1512
00:57:08,922 --> 00:57:10,361
- This is the maintenance office. - Right.

1513
00:57:10,362 --> 00:57:12,831
- After around a 600m walk, - Yes?

1514
00:57:12,832 --> 00:57:15,362
You can settle down at a camping spot of your choice.

1515
00:57:15,632 --> 00:57:17,162
So there's a place like that.

1516
00:57:17,902 --> 00:57:20,931
You'll find a much better view toward the oak tree forest.

1517
00:57:20,932 --> 00:57:22,671
- Understood. - Right.

1518
00:57:22,672 --> 00:57:23,941
- Have a great time. - Thank you.

1519
00:57:23,942 --> 00:57:25,101
(Thank you.)

1520
00:57:25,102 --> 00:57:26,542
Gosh.

1521
00:57:27,012 --> 00:57:31,042
(Full of excitement)

1522
00:57:34,252 --> 00:57:35,551
It's 600m away.

1523
00:57:35,552 --> 00:57:36,921
(Let's walk up 600m!)

1524
00:57:36,922 --> 00:57:39,752
Let's walk up 600m.

1525
00:57:42,362 --> 00:57:43,721
(Recreational Forest)

1526
00:57:43,722 --> 00:57:44,961
Up the path.

1527
00:57:44,962 --> 00:57:47,532
(I just need to walk up the path.)

1528
00:57:49,232 --> 00:57:50,362
Gosh, it smells nice.

1529
00:57:53,932 --> 00:57:55,032
Cosmos!

1530
00:57:55,702 --> 00:57:56,942
They're beautiful!

1531
00:57:57,502 --> 00:57:59,811
- They're beautiful! - It's great here.

1532
00:57:59,812 --> 00:58:00,812
Cosmos.

1533
00:58:01,612 --> 00:58:04,312
(The air and view are great.)

1534
00:58:05,812 --> 00:58:07,881
(Walking up)

1535
00:58:07,882 --> 00:58:09,482
(Panting)

1536
00:58:10,082 --> 00:58:11,282
You're a pretty one.

1537
00:58:12,892 --> 00:58:14,022
How pretty.

1538
00:58:15,192 --> 00:58:16,222
It's a dandelion.

1539
00:58:16,892 --> 00:58:17,892
(Panting)

1540
00:58:18,392 --> 00:58:19,592
Why are you so pretty?

1541
00:58:19,692 --> 00:58:22,131
How did just one flower pop out?

1542
00:58:22,132 --> 00:58:23,402
This is a banana tree.

1543
00:58:23,562 --> 00:58:25,872
- Bananas. - Your name says it all.

1544
00:58:26,572 --> 00:58:27,572
(Panting)

1545
00:58:27,802 --> 00:58:30,241
- You're slowly losing your breath. - Right?

1546
00:58:30,242 --> 00:58:31,672
She won't be talking as much now.

1547
00:58:35,642 --> 00:58:37,041
- What is that? - Gosh.

1548
00:58:37,042 --> 00:58:38,912
- What? - That can't be true.

1549
00:58:39,501 --> 00:58:41,381
(Ja Yeon is walking up to find the right spot.)

1550
00:58:41,911 --> 00:58:43,141
I still won't be halfway up.

1551
00:58:43,771 --> 00:58:44,781
(Panting)

1552
00:58:47,211 --> 00:58:48,350
- What is that? - Gosh.

1553
00:58:48,351 --> 00:58:50,411
- What? - That can't be true.

1554
00:58:51,551 --> 00:58:53,271
(She has a backpack that is as big as her.)

1555
00:58:53,621 --> 00:58:54,791
She's literally hiking.

1556
00:58:55,821 --> 00:58:56,891
That looks exhausting.

1557
00:58:57,191 --> 00:58:58,260
(Surprised)

1558
00:58:58,261 --> 00:58:59,290
Gosh, this is tough.

1559
00:58:59,291 --> 00:59:01,731
That must've been really tough.

1560
00:59:02,661 --> 00:59:05,901
Leaning back just a little bit would make her fall on her back.

1561
00:59:06,731 --> 00:59:09,101
I have to go up 600m?

1562
00:59:09,601 --> 00:59:11,071
(Panting)

1563
00:59:11,471 --> 00:59:13,071
Her breathing sounds a lot rougher.

1564
00:59:14,271 --> 00:59:15,970
It's like putting my body to the test.

1565
00:59:15,971 --> 00:59:17,340
That's part of the fun of it.

1566
00:59:17,341 --> 00:59:18,941
The fun of putting her body to the test.

1567
00:59:18,981 --> 00:59:20,640
Gosh, I can't go up!

1568
00:59:20,641 --> 00:59:22,511
(It's a very steep slope.)

1569
00:59:22,751 --> 00:59:24,421
It's a very steep slope.

1570
00:59:24,521 --> 00:59:26,750
That would be exhausting even without a backpack.

1571
00:59:26,751 --> 00:59:28,220
- Right. - Right.

1572
00:59:28,221 --> 00:59:29,491
It's like doing lunges.

1573
00:59:29,851 --> 00:59:32,991
What kind of people are those who go hiking?

1574
00:59:37,361 --> 00:59:39,760
- Look at that. - Ask for a ride!

1575
00:59:39,761 --> 00:59:40,830
Hitchhike!

1576
00:59:40,831 --> 00:59:42,571
(Letting out a breath)

1577
00:59:43,771 --> 00:59:45,570
(Panting)

1578
00:59:45,571 --> 00:59:47,441
Modern technology is moving by.

1579
00:59:48,971 --> 00:59:50,981
(Letting out a breath)

1580
00:59:51,741 --> 00:59:54,610
(I will go my way.)

1581
00:59:54,611 --> 00:59:56,081
It was very tough.

1582
00:59:56,581 --> 00:59:59,080
I felt the weight on my shoulders.

1583
00:59:59,081 --> 01:00:00,481
The weight of life.

1584
01:00:00,951 --> 01:00:03,390
- Right. - There's such a saying,

1585
01:00:03,391 --> 01:00:05,691
"Bearing the weight on one's shoulders."

1586
01:00:06,291 --> 01:00:07,691
I learned the true meaning of that.

1587
01:00:08,031 --> 01:00:09,031
(Panting)

1588
01:00:09,431 --> 01:00:10,461
(Stomach growling)

1589
01:00:11,231 --> 01:00:12,761
(I'm hungry and exhausted.)

1590
01:00:13,761 --> 01:00:16,171
(There seems to be no end to the slope.)

1591
01:00:18,971 --> 01:00:20,571
- What? - Is that the right way?

1592
01:00:20,841 --> 01:00:22,211
She has a hip-hop mindset.

1593
01:00:22,711 --> 01:00:25,441
(The final course of hardship was waiting for her.)

1594
01:00:26,481 --> 01:00:28,910
(Struggling)

1595
01:00:28,911 --> 01:00:30,311
That's the wild camping she wanted.

1596
01:00:32,421 --> 01:00:33,951
(Impressed)

1597
01:00:35,891 --> 01:00:37,090
- Nice. - It's this spot?

1598
01:00:37,091 --> 01:00:38,220
It's really nice.

1599
01:00:38,221 --> 01:00:39,490
(The oak tree forest unfolds after her suffering.)

1600
01:00:39,491 --> 01:00:41,291
- It's nice here. - This was the destination?

1601
01:00:43,761 --> 01:00:45,001
There are a lot of acorns.

1602
01:00:45,631 --> 01:00:47,151
This is certainly the oak tree forest.

1603
01:00:49,771 --> 01:00:52,271
There we go. I should look for a point.

1604
01:00:56,241 --> 01:00:59,981
(Feeling refreshed)

1605
01:01:01,581 --> 01:01:02,880
(Oak Tree Forest Observatory)

1606
01:01:02,881 --> 01:01:03,950
"Oak Tree Forest."

1607
01:01:03,951 --> 01:01:08,121
(A place hidden in nature)

1608
01:01:08,891 --> 01:01:10,420
It does look really nice.

1609
01:01:10,421 --> 01:01:11,960
- It's really nice. - It was great.

1610
01:01:11,961 --> 01:01:13,020
(Full of the smell of fall)

1611
01:01:13,021 --> 01:01:14,691
- Pine cones. - It's really nice.

1612
01:01:16,131 --> 01:01:19,031
Let's look for a point.

1613
01:01:19,561 --> 01:01:21,331
Are there no other tents?

1614
01:01:21,471 --> 01:01:22,700
It's first come, first served.

1615
01:01:22,701 --> 01:01:24,400
Taking the best spot first...

1616
01:01:24,401 --> 01:01:26,370
- is the most important. - Right.

1617
01:01:26,371 --> 01:01:28,011
- Right. - It's first come, first served?

1618
01:01:28,411 --> 01:01:30,811
(Amazed)

1619
01:01:34,581 --> 01:01:35,910
(This spot is great.)

1620
01:01:35,911 --> 01:01:38,021
- This is it! - It's nice here.

1621
01:01:38,481 --> 01:01:39,851
This is an excellent spot.

1622
01:01:42,721 --> 01:01:44,051
Gosh.

1623
01:01:44,251 --> 01:01:46,261
There should be a nice view.

1624
01:01:49,461 --> 01:01:50,831
Which way is this facing?

1625
01:01:52,001 --> 01:01:55,031
(Let's take a look.)

1626
01:01:56,331 --> 01:01:57,471
Southwest.

1627
01:01:58,971 --> 01:02:00,140
Southwest.

1628
01:02:00,141 --> 01:02:01,611
(It's a good direction as well.)

1629
01:02:05,341 --> 01:02:07,950
- The leaves are changing color. - It feels a bit like "Exhuma."

1630
01:02:07,951 --> 01:02:09,511
- It gives off those vibes. - "Exhuma."

1631
01:02:09,751 --> 01:02:11,821
She does feel like a guru from behind.

1632
01:02:12,321 --> 01:02:14,891
She would have a white beard when she turns around.

1633
01:02:15,191 --> 01:02:16,290
Not a woman?

1634
01:02:16,291 --> 01:02:18,160
- Yes, it's just how it feels. - The vibe.

1635
01:02:18,161 --> 01:02:19,390
She was a natural person.

1636
01:02:19,391 --> 01:02:22,190
You know how people wear a ponytail in Cheonghak-dong Village.

1637
01:02:22,191 --> 01:02:23,830
- That's right. - A ponytail?

1638
01:02:23,831 --> 01:02:25,560
- The original ponytails. - Of course.

1639
01:02:25,561 --> 01:02:26,630
They'd have a long beard...

1640
01:02:26,631 --> 01:02:27,900
- when they turn around. - That's right.

1641
01:02:27,901 --> 01:02:29,861
(They'd have a long beard when they turn around.)

1642
01:02:34,711 --> 01:02:36,171
It's so vast.

1643
01:02:37,071 --> 01:02:39,081
I look like someone who came to buy a property.

1644
01:02:39,481 --> 01:02:41,151
There are some rocks on the ground.

1645
01:02:41,951 --> 01:02:43,681
But it's not too bad to sleep here.

1646
01:02:45,451 --> 01:02:48,551
(Shall I begin now?)

1647
01:02:50,191 --> 01:02:53,621
(Fluttering)

1648
01:02:55,791 --> 01:02:59,631
(Now that she's picked a spot, she starts setting up the tent.)

1649
01:03:01,701 --> 01:03:03,770
- She's doing it for real. - How does she do that?

1650
01:03:03,771 --> 01:03:04,941
(Fascinated)

1651
01:03:07,301 --> 01:03:08,970
(Skillfully unfolding the poles...)

1652
01:03:08,971 --> 01:03:11,881
(and pitching the tent smoothly)

1653
01:03:12,611 --> 01:03:15,150
(She pitches the tent in an instant.)

1654
01:03:15,151 --> 01:03:17,711
- She sets it up so fast. - Gosh, are you done already?

1655
01:03:18,021 --> 01:03:19,150
Yes, I just need a cover.

1656
01:03:19,151 --> 01:03:20,221
That didn't take long.

1657
01:03:22,151 --> 01:03:24,151
But... Gosh, she's so good.

1658
01:03:25,721 --> 01:03:26,891
She set it up so fast.

1659
01:03:27,591 --> 01:03:28,837
Camping must be fun since she's good.

1660
01:03:28,861 --> 01:03:29,861
(Hitting)

1661
01:03:30,231 --> 01:03:32,430
Gosh, she hammers it with a rock.

1662
01:03:32,431 --> 01:03:33,911
(She hammers the stakes with a rock.)

1663
01:03:35,671 --> 01:03:37,000
(The 1-person tent is set up in no time.)

1664
01:03:37,001 --> 01:03:39,000
- She's really good. - How can she do it so skillfully?

1665
01:03:39,001 --> 01:03:40,100
(The 1-person tent is set up in no time.)

1666
01:03:40,101 --> 01:03:41,711
She's really good.

1667
01:03:42,241 --> 01:03:44,880
We've only seen people who don't know how to set up a tent.

1668
01:03:44,881 --> 01:03:45,881
We finally got to see...

1669
01:03:45,882 --> 01:03:47,180
- the real deal. - Someone who does it for real.

1670
01:03:47,181 --> 01:03:49,211
We finally have the real deal.

1671
01:03:49,851 --> 01:03:54,121
(The real camper keeps busy without resting.)

1672
01:03:55,991 --> 01:03:57,091
(Rumbling)

1673
01:03:57,491 --> 01:03:58,661
Gosh, I'm hungry.

1674
01:04:00,091 --> 01:04:01,490
(It's already 1:30pm. Her stomach is growling like thunder.)

1675
01:04:01,491 --> 01:04:03,690
- She must be hungry. - You should eat something fast.

1676
01:04:03,691 --> 01:04:05,401
Your stomach is making animal noises.

1677
01:04:06,161 --> 01:04:07,401
It's roaring.

1678
01:04:07,561 --> 01:04:08,631
I'm starving.

1679
01:04:10,331 --> 01:04:12,471
(Let's set it up quickly.)

1680
01:04:13,341 --> 01:04:14,941
Yes, let's eat first.

1681
01:04:15,341 --> 01:04:17,341
(She sets up a camping chair in no time.)

1682
01:04:18,181 --> 01:04:19,441
(Pulling)

1683
01:04:19,941 --> 01:04:21,251
All of them must be light.

1684
01:04:21,351 --> 01:04:23,151
- They are. - They're really small.

1685
01:04:24,981 --> 01:04:28,621
(Setting up smoothly)

1686
01:04:31,961 --> 01:04:33,921
That's really small and cute.

1687
01:04:34,661 --> 01:04:36,830
(Opening...)

1688
01:04:36,831 --> 01:04:38,031
(and opening again)

1689
01:04:38,661 --> 01:04:41,601
(If you fix the back...)

1690
01:04:42,271 --> 01:04:43,870
- A stove... - They're so cute.

1691
01:04:43,871 --> 01:04:45,001
Oh, that's a pot stand?

1692
01:04:45,401 --> 01:04:46,770
There are so many things in her backpack.

1693
01:04:46,771 --> 01:04:48,840
I have everything in there. I have a house and a kitchen too.

1694
01:04:48,841 --> 01:04:50,671
Exactly. That's so...

1695
01:04:51,411 --> 01:04:53,240
That's totally science.

1696
01:04:53,241 --> 01:04:55,121
(How much the backpack can hold is scientific.)

1697
01:04:55,381 --> 01:04:56,481
I'm so hungry.

1698
01:04:58,781 --> 01:05:00,551
(She washes her hands clean.)

1699
01:05:05,221 --> 01:05:06,321
What is that?

1700
01:05:06,691 --> 01:05:07,790
Are they beans?

1701
01:05:07,791 --> 01:05:08,991
- Beans? - Red beans?

1702
01:05:09,161 --> 01:05:10,230
Yes, they're red beans.

1703
01:05:10,231 --> 01:05:11,391
Why did you put in red beans?

1704
01:05:12,531 --> 01:05:15,471
(Pouring water into the pot with red beans)

1705
01:05:18,471 --> 01:05:21,000
(She smoothly sets up the stove.)

1706
01:05:21,001 --> 01:05:22,641
That can be used in the mountains, right?

1707
01:05:22,711 --> 01:05:25,681
The lunch menu is the dish of my dream for camping.

1708
01:05:26,311 --> 01:05:27,541
It's red bean kalguksu.

1709
01:05:28,411 --> 01:05:29,910
- Goodness. - Why make red bean kalguksu...

1710
01:05:29,911 --> 01:05:31,420
in the mountains?

1711
01:05:31,421 --> 01:05:32,681
Red bean kalguksu?

1712
01:05:32,881 --> 01:05:35,621
- You soaked the beans in advance. - Yes, I did the day before.

1713
01:05:36,651 --> 01:05:38,497
- That takes a lot of work. - What about the noodles?

1714
01:05:38,521 --> 01:05:40,290
- I should roll out the dough. - What?

1715
01:05:40,291 --> 01:05:41,691
You're rolling out the dough there?

1716
01:05:42,291 --> 01:05:43,571
Did you open a restaurant there?

1717
01:05:44,831 --> 01:05:45,961
Is that even possible?

1718
01:05:46,331 --> 01:05:47,560
Normally, people bring food...

1719
01:05:47,561 --> 01:05:48,770
- that's easy to make. - That's right.

1720
01:05:48,771 --> 01:05:50,811
- Like ones you can just boil. - Like budae jjigae.

1721
01:05:50,901 --> 01:05:53,171
- Or something you can just grill. - That's right.

1722
01:05:53,341 --> 01:05:57,111
But going camping made me want to try new things.

1723
01:05:57,241 --> 01:05:58,411
Various menus.

1724
01:05:59,711 --> 01:06:01,850
(That's how she challenged herself into making red bean kalguksu.)

1725
01:06:01,851 --> 01:06:02,951
That looks fun, though.

1726
01:06:03,681 --> 01:06:07,151
As for noodles, I'm using whole wheat flour.

1727
01:06:07,351 --> 01:06:11,361
The goal is to make red bean kalguksu of my own.

1728
01:06:12,221 --> 01:06:15,261
- Just like making acorn jelly. - Right, she made acorn jelly too.

1729
01:06:15,991 --> 01:06:18,660
- She's good with food like that. - That's right.

1730
01:06:18,661 --> 01:06:21,000
You have mastered traditional dishes.

1731
01:06:21,001 --> 01:06:23,271
- She's like a master. - A master, yes.

1732
01:06:25,701 --> 01:06:27,740
(As a master, she prepares a healthy meal.)

1733
01:06:27,741 --> 01:06:29,941
She eats healthy food even while camping.

1734
01:06:35,711 --> 01:06:37,751
You brought the bag to make it easy.

1735
01:06:38,281 --> 01:06:39,451
Yes, right in that bag.

1736
01:06:46,221 --> 01:06:49,361
(Kneading)

1737
01:06:50,791 --> 01:06:54,031
(She can't give up the homemade touch even while camping.)

1738
01:06:55,171 --> 01:06:57,031
It looks like a food journey.

1739
01:06:58,641 --> 01:06:59,700
This is fun.

1740
01:06:59,701 --> 01:07:01,441
(Ok Ja Yeon's Traditional Food Journey)

1741
01:07:06,911 --> 01:07:08,911
(Kneading gently with the touch of a master)

1742
01:07:12,981 --> 01:07:14,950
Oh, she's using the bottle as a roller.

1743
01:07:14,951 --> 01:07:16,150
That's the fun of it.

1744
01:07:16,151 --> 01:07:17,351
Humans use tools.

1745
01:07:23,331 --> 01:07:24,631
She rolled it pretty well.

1746
01:07:25,261 --> 01:07:28,501
(Rolling the dough for a while)

1747
01:07:31,271 --> 01:07:33,671
It felt really good. The dough rolled really well.

1748
01:07:34,341 --> 01:07:37,071
I cut the dough, and it was really smooth.

1749
01:07:37,141 --> 01:07:39,811
I felt like the noodles would turn out well. I was really excited.

1750
01:07:40,411 --> 01:07:42,151
Doing that...

1751
01:07:42,651 --> 01:07:45,951
in nature felt out of place, and it was so much fun.

1752
01:07:46,451 --> 01:07:49,551
It felt like the tall trees were watching me.

1753
01:07:50,091 --> 01:07:51,790
It felt like the birds were watching too.

1754
01:07:51,791 --> 01:07:54,721
It was like I was having fun playing house.

1755
01:07:56,291 --> 01:07:57,431
I get what it feels like.

1756
01:07:59,361 --> 01:08:00,931
It's like "Little Forest."

1757
01:08:04,171 --> 01:08:05,441
The noodles turned out well.

1758
01:08:06,101 --> 01:08:07,940
- It's in such good quality. - Yes.

1759
01:08:07,941 --> 01:08:09,741
- Not bad, right? - It turned out well.

1760
01:08:11,071 --> 01:08:13,281
(After making whole wheat noodles...)

1761
01:08:18,281 --> 01:08:21,051
(Thinking)

1762
01:08:23,291 --> 01:08:24,321
What?

1763
01:08:28,361 --> 01:08:30,030
Are you making chopsticks out of that?

1764
01:08:30,031 --> 01:08:31,091
That's right.

1765
01:08:32,861 --> 01:08:34,461
It's my second dream.

1766
01:08:35,601 --> 01:08:37,331
In YouTube videos,

1767
01:08:37,931 --> 01:08:40,170
they make chopsticks out of wood...

1768
01:08:40,171 --> 01:08:42,140
that fell off.

1769
01:08:42,141 --> 01:08:43,411
(It's called wood carving.)

1770
01:08:43,771 --> 01:08:46,141
I thought of giving it a try,

1771
01:08:46,311 --> 01:08:48,380
so I bought a carving knife.

1772
01:08:48,381 --> 01:08:50,910
And so, I brought it today with ambition.

1773
01:08:50,911 --> 01:08:52,850
The ones who are good at that are amazing.

1774
01:08:52,851 --> 01:08:54,081
They even make bowls too.

1775
01:08:54,891 --> 01:08:56,121
She's like Geppetto.

1776
01:08:56,291 --> 01:08:57,421
Or Okppetto.

1777
01:08:57,691 --> 01:08:58,891
Okppetto?

1778
01:08:59,391 --> 01:09:01,731
(Scraping)

1779
01:09:01,831 --> 01:09:04,631
(Carving)

1780
01:09:05,631 --> 01:09:06,660
(Master Okppetto only carves wood without speaking.)

1781
01:09:06,661 --> 01:09:08,631
It took really long.

1782
01:09:09,031 --> 01:09:11,131
Even though it was a thin piece of wood...

1783
01:09:13,141 --> 01:09:14,371
It's too short.

1784
01:09:15,771 --> 01:09:16,970
(She kept on carving for a while.)

1785
01:09:16,971 --> 01:09:18,641
That's the fun of it.

1786
01:09:19,311 --> 01:09:21,111
- There's nothing else to do either. - Right.

1787
01:09:22,011 --> 01:09:23,451
My right arm will be thicker.

1788
01:09:27,981 --> 01:09:29,091
They've become really thin.

1789
01:09:30,051 --> 01:09:31,690
- She should open a studio. - You can even take them home.

1790
01:09:31,691 --> 01:09:33,160
- That's usable. - You should take them home.

1791
01:09:33,161 --> 01:09:34,321
They've become really thin.

1792
01:09:35,561 --> 01:09:38,901
(Washing her hands as she touched wood)

1793
01:09:40,931 --> 01:09:42,171
Oh, I can crush them now.

1794
01:09:43,571 --> 01:09:45,301
It's crushable now.

1795
01:09:48,141 --> 01:09:50,371
- They turned out totally nice. - That looks good.

1796
01:09:51,111 --> 01:09:54,011
(Putting it in another container)

1797
01:09:54,841 --> 01:09:58,981
(Then she adds water to make a red bean porridge.)

1798
01:10:00,221 --> 01:10:02,921
- That's why she brought salt. - The one she packed separately.

1799
01:10:03,321 --> 01:10:04,391
Sweet and salty.

1800
01:10:06,461 --> 01:10:07,860
She's making all that...

1801
01:10:07,861 --> 01:10:09,301
- with those small tools. - Exactly.

1802
01:10:14,561 --> 01:10:16,070
(Adding whole wheat noodles into the water...)

1803
01:10:16,071 --> 01:10:17,531
(that boiled red beans)

1804
01:10:18,131 --> 01:10:19,740
So she's cooking the noodles first.

1805
01:10:19,741 --> 01:10:21,071
She's amazing.

1806
01:10:24,371 --> 01:10:25,510
(Stirring with the chopsticks she just made)

1807
01:10:25,511 --> 01:10:26,640
Look at the chopsticks.

1808
01:10:26,641 --> 01:10:28,321
- She did a good job. - It's wood carving.

1809
01:10:30,211 --> 01:10:31,381
They all break easily.

1810
01:10:32,881 --> 01:10:34,081
They all break easily.

1811
01:10:34,991 --> 01:10:36,051
Goodness.

1812
01:10:36,251 --> 01:10:38,591
You should've kneaded it more.

1813
01:10:39,821 --> 01:10:40,890
Darn it.

1814
01:10:40,891 --> 01:10:41,891
(Oh, no...)

1815
01:10:43,331 --> 01:10:45,131
They all broke off the moment I touched them.

1816
01:10:46,261 --> 01:10:47,400
They're too short.

1817
01:10:47,401 --> 01:10:50,571
(Let's add them and boil first.)

1818
01:10:51,571 --> 01:10:52,971
This isn't kalguksu.

1819
01:10:58,841 --> 01:10:59,940
But it looks pretty good.

1820
01:10:59,941 --> 01:11:01,581
It'd have been amazing if she had kimchi.

1821
01:11:01,751 --> 01:11:03,551
- That'd be perfect. - It looks good, though.

1822
01:11:04,111 --> 01:11:06,021
I know because I make pasta.

1823
01:11:06,221 --> 01:11:08,120
You should've added eggs.

1824
01:11:08,121 --> 01:11:09,821
Whole wheat noodles break more easily.

1825
01:11:10,021 --> 01:11:12,260
It was my first time making noodles.

1826
01:11:12,261 --> 01:11:13,420
You can think of it as...

1827
01:11:13,421 --> 01:11:14,590
- whole wheat sujebi. - Of course.

1828
01:11:14,591 --> 01:11:16,561
- You cut them as you eat anyway. - Of course.

1829
01:11:16,791 --> 01:11:18,631
- As long as it's delicious. - Yes, of course.

1830
01:11:18,801 --> 01:11:20,700
She didn't get to use the chopsticks in the end.

1831
01:11:20,701 --> 01:11:23,170
You're right.

1832
01:11:23,171 --> 01:11:25,070
- I couldn't use the chopsticks. - She couldn't.

1833
01:11:25,071 --> 01:11:27,601
- It's all right. - I had to use a spoon to scoop it.

1834
01:11:32,811 --> 01:11:34,381
It's 3:47pm.

1835
01:11:34,641 --> 01:11:37,581
It took you 3 hours. You started at 1pm when you got there.

1836
01:11:38,981 --> 01:11:41,321
(Her lunch is ready after a long journey of three hours.)

1837
01:11:42,151 --> 01:11:43,751
(Blowing)

1838
01:11:44,321 --> 01:11:46,690
(A deeply moving first bite)

1839
01:11:46,691 --> 01:11:48,791
That kalguksu must cost a lot.

1840
01:11:48,931 --> 01:11:50,431
She should get 100 dollars for it.

1841
01:11:53,001 --> 01:11:54,060
How did it taste?

1842
01:11:54,061 --> 01:11:55,830
Gosh, anything must taste good.

1843
01:11:55,831 --> 01:11:57,511
She'd enjoy it even if it's not delicious.

1844
01:12:01,071 --> 01:12:02,711
I can't say it's delicious.

1845
01:12:03,441 --> 01:12:04,570
When you were starving?

1846
01:12:04,571 --> 01:12:06,341
- No. - How come?

1847
01:12:06,481 --> 01:12:08,550
It's because the whole wheat noodles...

1848
01:12:08,551 --> 01:12:09,981
You didn't season it a little?

1849
01:12:10,281 --> 01:12:12,851
I should've added salt while making the noodles.

1850
01:12:13,121 --> 01:12:14,150
So you didn't add salt.

1851
01:12:14,151 --> 01:12:15,751
The noodles didn't mix well.

1852
01:12:16,291 --> 01:12:18,021
The noodles weren't good.

1853
01:12:18,191 --> 01:12:19,490
- It's hard. - Ja Yeon...

1854
01:12:19,491 --> 01:12:21,121
Ja Yeon, the food you make...

1855
01:12:21,261 --> 01:12:22,931
isn't that appetizing.

1856
01:12:24,091 --> 01:12:25,930
- Didn't you say she was good? - What did you say?

1857
01:12:25,931 --> 01:12:28,430
They look like they'd help you lose weight.

1858
01:12:28,431 --> 01:12:29,730
- That's not true. - You can't say that...

1859
01:12:29,731 --> 01:12:31,247
as someone who made that horrible kimchi fried rice.

1860
01:12:31,271 --> 01:12:32,900
I feel like I'm a better cook.

1861
01:12:32,901 --> 01:12:34,140
- No! - My gosh.

1862
01:12:34,141 --> 01:12:35,570
- This is... - I'm quite good too.

1863
01:12:35,571 --> 01:12:37,440
That's because it was my first time making noodles.

1864
01:12:37,441 --> 01:12:39,310
You should really reflect on yourself to hear that from Kian84.

1865
01:12:39,311 --> 01:12:40,840
- That's... - No.

1866
01:12:40,841 --> 01:12:42,810
- It's too... - He's not even listening.

1867
01:12:42,811 --> 01:12:45,150
It's leaning to healthy food too much.

1868
01:12:45,151 --> 01:12:47,080
- You gave up on the taste. - But it's good...

1869
01:12:47,081 --> 01:12:48,790
- She sounds really upset. - It took me three hours.

1870
01:12:48,791 --> 01:12:50,750
I've never seen her get this worked up.

1871
01:12:50,751 --> 01:12:51,920
I'm sweating.

1872
01:12:51,921 --> 01:12:53,360
That's not true.

1873
01:12:53,361 --> 01:12:54,960
Have a cooking competition with him with a time limit.

1874
01:12:54,961 --> 01:12:56,460
- You should. - Let them compete,

1875
01:12:56,461 --> 01:12:57,630
- and we can have a blind test. - I'd make noodles again.

1876
01:12:57,631 --> 01:12:59,460
- Wait. You're going to eat it? - What?

1877
01:12:59,461 --> 01:13:01,477
- You're going to eat it? - I can just taste it a little.

1878
01:13:01,501 --> 01:13:02,971
I'll show you my skills.

1879
01:13:10,311 --> 01:13:12,241
I'm just eating it since I made it.

1880
01:13:16,451 --> 01:13:19,351
(Consuming it with a heavy responsibility)

1881
01:13:19,821 --> 01:13:21,181
Why am I sad?

1882
01:13:26,361 --> 01:13:28,491
(Cawing)

1883
01:13:30,091 --> 01:13:32,431
- Crows are cawing. - Even they won't eat that.

1884
01:13:34,101 --> 01:13:36,471
(Cawing)

1885
01:13:36,771 --> 01:13:38,051
They're kind of laughing at you.

1886
01:13:38,701 --> 01:13:40,501
Why does it feel like they're laughing at me?

1887
01:13:42,311 --> 01:13:43,871
(Cawing)

1888
01:13:46,681 --> 01:13:48,111
I should make dinner.

1889
01:13:50,081 --> 01:13:52,250
You're making dinner right away?

1890
01:13:52,251 --> 01:13:53,851
I had to. It was late.

1891
01:13:54,181 --> 01:13:56,351
You should have something delicious.

1892
01:13:58,821 --> 01:14:01,521
The sun has gone down a lot.

1893
01:14:03,691 --> 01:14:06,231
(Rustling)

1894
01:14:06,961 --> 01:14:08,721
(Ja Yeon takes something out from the tent.)

1895
01:14:09,671 --> 01:14:10,771
Food?

1896
01:14:11,771 --> 01:14:12,800
A cutting board?

1897
01:14:12,801 --> 01:14:15,411
Since the sun is setting,

1898
01:14:15,911 --> 01:14:18,241
it's a little cold.

1899
01:14:18,911 --> 01:14:21,710
You have a kitchen, a bed, a house,

1900
01:14:21,711 --> 01:14:23,631
- and a closet in that backpack. - And a closet.

1901
01:14:26,521 --> 01:14:28,321
(She sits down after putting on a jacket.)

1902
01:14:32,721 --> 01:14:33,721
(Putting it down)

1903
01:14:33,921 --> 01:14:35,091
Is that a cutting board?

1904
01:14:35,261 --> 01:14:36,861
- It looks like it. - A cutting board...

1905
01:14:39,061 --> 01:14:40,931
- What is it? - What is it?

1906
01:14:41,501 --> 01:14:44,271
Recently, my friend who does plays...

1907
01:14:44,471 --> 01:14:47,000
said they pressed flowers because the flowers they received...

1908
01:14:47,001 --> 01:14:48,871
withered really fast.

1909
01:14:49,171 --> 01:14:51,111
- I see. - Press flowers?

1910
01:14:52,481 --> 01:14:55,551
(The flower petals we kept between pages when we were young)

1911
01:14:56,351 --> 01:14:57,680
I see.

1912
01:14:57,681 --> 01:14:59,180
Didn't you all have one in a frame?

1913
01:14:59,181 --> 01:15:00,181
Of course.

1914
01:15:00,182 --> 01:15:01,897
I used to do that a lot when I was younger.

1915
01:15:01,921 --> 01:15:03,197
I haven't seen that for a long while.

1916
01:15:03,221 --> 01:15:04,820
They said if you press just one piece,

1917
01:15:04,821 --> 01:15:07,561
you can remember the bouquet.

1918
01:15:08,021 --> 01:15:11,691
I thought I should make one too.

1919
01:15:11,791 --> 01:15:13,231
Then I came camping,

1920
01:15:13,501 --> 01:15:15,370
and I thought making that could help me remember...

1921
01:15:15,371 --> 01:15:17,201
the campsites I've been to.

1922
01:15:17,471 --> 01:15:19,440
That's why I thought of pressing flowers.

1923
01:15:19,441 --> 01:15:21,771
- That's romantic. - That really is romantic.

1924
01:15:24,941 --> 01:15:26,821
There weren't any pretty leaves from this tree.

1925
01:15:28,411 --> 01:15:32,051
It's an oak grove, but there weren't any pretty oak leaves.

1926
01:15:33,681 --> 01:15:35,450
It was an oak grove,

1927
01:15:35,451 --> 01:15:38,691
so I wanted to pick a pretty oak leaf on the ground.

1928
01:15:39,261 --> 01:15:41,591
But only the insect-eaten ones fell on the ground.

1929
01:15:45,431 --> 01:15:47,500
(Putting the leaves together carefully...)

1930
01:15:47,501 --> 01:15:49,571
(with the flower pressing kit)

1931
01:15:49,601 --> 01:15:51,430
Oh, there's a kit for it?

1932
01:15:51,431 --> 01:15:53,201
What's this? How fascinating.

1933
01:15:56,111 --> 01:15:57,570
And then...

1934
01:15:57,571 --> 01:15:58,680
(Putting a paper and a wooden board on the flowers)

1935
01:15:58,681 --> 01:15:59,811
Press it.

1936
01:16:00,581 --> 01:16:02,581
I guess you must tighten it with a screw.

1937
01:16:02,851 --> 01:16:04,510
It's fascinating.

1938
01:16:04,511 --> 01:16:06,820
Oh, like that?

1939
01:16:06,821 --> 01:16:08,721
That really is romantic.

1940
01:16:09,391 --> 01:16:11,451
Oh, you can press it this hard.

1941
01:16:12,221 --> 01:16:13,620
We used to use a paper dictionary...

1942
01:16:13,621 --> 01:16:15,060
- to press. - That's right.

1943
01:16:15,061 --> 01:16:16,261
That's right.

1944
01:16:16,931 --> 01:16:18,801
I wonder how it'll turn out.

1945
01:16:19,301 --> 01:16:20,330
Can that be made in a day?

1946
01:16:20,331 --> 01:16:22,070
- How long does it take? - I think it needs a few days.

1947
01:16:22,071 --> 01:16:23,371
At least three days.

1948
01:16:23,831 --> 01:16:25,500
It's been 3 to 4 days.

1949
01:16:25,501 --> 01:16:27,300
- You brought it? Gosh. - Oh, my.

1950
01:16:27,301 --> 01:16:29,410
You're seeing the result for the first time with us?

1951
01:16:29,411 --> 01:16:30,910
- Yes, I don't know either. - Really?

1952
01:16:30,911 --> 01:16:32,587
- I don't know if it worked or not. - Oh, my.

1953
01:16:32,611 --> 01:16:34,041
Did it turn out okay?

1954
01:16:34,211 --> 01:16:35,551
- It must be pretty. - All right.

1955
01:16:35,751 --> 01:16:38,051
I'm nervous as I ruined red bean kalguksu as well.

1956
01:16:38,351 --> 01:16:39,680
- Don't be nervous. - Let's see.

1957
01:16:39,681 --> 01:16:40,720
(All eyes on her)

1958
01:16:40,721 --> 01:16:42,561
We're seeing this together for the first time.

1959
01:16:43,051 --> 01:16:44,551
(Amazed)

1960
01:16:44,969 --> 01:16:47,209
(The pressed flowers Ja Yeon made when she went camping)

1961
01:16:48,109 --> 01:16:49,148
- Drumroll. - We're seeing this together...

1962
01:16:49,149 --> 01:16:50,149
for the first time.

1963
01:16:51,179 --> 01:16:52,348
(Amazed)

1964
01:16:52,349 --> 01:16:54,018
- It's pretty. - It turned out really well.

1965
01:16:54,019 --> 01:16:55,289
One by one.

1966
01:16:56,148 --> 01:16:58,948
(The flowers and leaves were dried beautifully.)

1967
01:16:59,478 --> 01:17:01,847
- It looks like it's embroidered. - The flowers are pretty.

1968
01:17:01,848 --> 01:17:02,947
- I know. It looks like a brooch. - It's so pretty.

1969
01:17:02,948 --> 01:17:04,428
It looks like a pin for your clothes.

1970
01:17:04,618 --> 01:17:06,518
The flowers turned out better than I thought.

1971
01:17:06,588 --> 01:17:08,257
- They turned out really nicely. - The flowers turned out well.

1972
01:17:08,258 --> 01:17:10,034
- The flowers turned out well. - The flowers are pretty.

1973
01:17:10,058 --> 01:17:11,087
How pretty.

1974
01:17:11,088 --> 01:17:12,927
- The flowers are pretty. - The flowers are pretty.

1975
01:17:12,928 --> 01:17:13,958
I think...

1976
01:17:14,398 --> 01:17:16,267
it'll be better to laminate it.

1977
01:17:16,268 --> 01:17:17,467
Are you really giving them out?

1978
01:17:17,468 --> 01:17:18,898
- Yes. - You're giving them to us?

1979
01:17:19,098 --> 01:17:20,098
Yes.

1980
01:17:20,298 --> 01:17:21,368
- Really? - Yes.

1981
01:17:21,438 --> 01:17:22,638
(Smiling)

1982
01:17:22,738 --> 01:17:24,437
- Won't they break? - But this...

1983
01:17:24,438 --> 01:17:25,537
We should keep it in a book.

1984
01:17:25,538 --> 01:17:27,807
- Sounds good. - This one's really pretty.

1985
01:17:27,808 --> 01:17:28,808
Let's keep it in the wallet.

1986
01:17:28,809 --> 01:17:30,207
- It's so fascinating. - It's pretty.

1987
01:17:30,208 --> 01:17:31,817
- It's really fascinating. - This is really pretty.

1988
01:17:31,818 --> 01:17:32,948
It smells nice.

1989
01:17:33,478 --> 01:17:34,718
(Feeling proud)

1990
01:17:36,488 --> 01:17:38,117
We got to see nice things thanks to you.

1991
01:17:38,118 --> 01:17:39,388
It's really fascinating.

1992
01:17:39,618 --> 01:17:41,688
(Thinking)

1993
01:17:42,328 --> 01:17:44,228
It feels like I just finished eating, but...

1994
01:17:45,528 --> 01:17:47,597
I need to make dinner before the sun sets.

1995
01:17:47,598 --> 01:17:49,468
- Gosh. - You need to move quickly.

1996
01:17:50,828 --> 01:17:52,298
(Rustling)

1997
01:17:53,468 --> 01:17:55,207
(She takes out a big bag from the backpack.)

1998
01:17:55,208 --> 01:17:56,308
What is that?

1999
01:17:57,238 --> 01:17:58,267
(Jeju red tilefish)

2000
01:17:58,268 --> 01:17:59,838
- Red tilefish? - Red tilefish?

2001
01:17:59,908 --> 01:18:01,238
You brought red tilefish?

2002
01:18:02,178 --> 01:18:03,377
For dinner,

2003
01:18:03,378 --> 01:18:06,317
I'm making red tilefish over rice and Jeju-style red tilefish soup.

2004
01:18:06,318 --> 01:18:07,777
- Red tilefish over rice? - Gosh.

2005
01:18:07,778 --> 01:18:08,848
Red tilefish?

2006
01:18:09,488 --> 01:18:11,687
I told you I went camping on Jeju Island, right?

2007
01:18:11,688 --> 01:18:14,487
I bought a huge red tilefish then...

2008
01:18:14,488 --> 01:18:15,658
and grilled it.

2009
01:18:16,658 --> 01:18:18,057
It was so good.

2010
01:18:18,058 --> 01:18:20,028
Since then, I've ordered red tilefish.

2011
01:18:20,128 --> 01:18:22,567
I made red tilefish over rice with the one I ordered...

2012
01:18:22,568 --> 01:18:24,828
when I went camping last time, and it was really good.

2013
01:18:25,028 --> 01:18:28,538
So I thought I should make red tilefish soup as well this time.

2014
01:18:29,008 --> 01:18:31,308
Amazingly delicious red tilefish meal.

2015
01:18:32,138 --> 01:18:33,738
Amazingly delicious red tilefish meal.

2016
01:18:35,038 --> 01:18:37,548
(Taking out ingredients for the amazingly delicious dinner)

2017
01:18:39,078 --> 01:18:40,677
That's why you packed seasoning.

2018
01:18:40,678 --> 01:18:43,439
Yes, that's how you should pack. You can't bring new ones every time.

2019
01:18:44,948 --> 01:18:46,758
(Heating up the pot first)

2020
01:18:47,318 --> 01:18:49,328
(Kelp in rice?)

2021
01:18:49,458 --> 01:18:53,098
First, bring the rice and kelp to a full boil.

2022
01:18:53,998 --> 01:18:55,368
(Grabbing)

2023
01:18:56,468 --> 01:18:58,798
(Focused)

2024
01:19:00,438 --> 01:19:03,038
(Cutting it skillfully)

2025
01:19:04,408 --> 01:19:06,278
- Is it half-dried? - Yes.

2026
01:19:08,778 --> 01:19:10,878
(Spreading the red tilefish that she cut)

2027
01:19:11,808 --> 01:19:13,717
- What's that? - It's sesame oil.

2028
01:19:13,718 --> 01:19:14,878
- I see. - I see.

2029
01:19:16,188 --> 01:19:17,547
(Applying sesame oil thoroughly)

2030
01:19:17,548 --> 01:19:18,588
All set.

2031
01:19:22,228 --> 01:19:24,957
- Oh, you grill it. - She grills it first.

2032
01:19:24,958 --> 01:19:26,357
- Gosh. - You're amazing.

2033
01:19:26,358 --> 01:19:27,428
That looks delicious.

2034
01:19:32,438 --> 01:19:34,537
(Golden brown)

2035
01:19:34,538 --> 01:19:35,708
That looks good.

2036
01:19:36,138 --> 01:19:38,268
(I hope it grills well.)

2037
01:19:40,038 --> 01:19:43,048
(Grabbing a handful of scallions while the red tilefish is grilled)

2038
01:19:44,248 --> 01:19:46,348
(Cutting it into an edible size)

2039
01:19:47,548 --> 01:19:49,288
(Checking)

2040
01:19:50,588 --> 01:19:53,188
When the water boils down,

2041
01:19:53,558 --> 01:19:55,358
lower it to medium heat.

2042
01:19:55,458 --> 01:19:57,157
Add scallions to the rice.

2043
01:19:57,158 --> 01:19:58,557
Oh, to the rice?

2044
01:19:58,558 --> 01:19:59,627
(Spreading scallions on the rice)

2045
01:19:59,628 --> 01:20:01,528
You can't give up on scallions.

2046
01:20:02,468 --> 01:20:05,397
(Adding the well-grilled red tilefish on top)

2047
01:20:05,398 --> 01:20:07,097
- My gosh. - That looks really good.

2048
01:20:07,098 --> 01:20:10,707
Let it steam for a while and have it.

2049
01:20:10,708 --> 01:20:11,837
Red tilefish over rice is really good.

2050
01:20:11,838 --> 01:20:12,978
It must be so good.

2051
01:20:15,078 --> 01:20:17,208
(Red tilefish with bones is put into the kettle.)

2052
01:20:17,948 --> 01:20:19,677
- Side without bones is for rice. - That's right.

2053
01:20:19,678 --> 01:20:21,047
- Side with bones is for soup. - Soup.

2054
01:20:21,048 --> 01:20:22,788
- It's perfect. - Gosh.

2055
01:20:23,888 --> 01:20:26,368
(Cutting the radish and chillies she brought into a bite size)

2056
01:20:27,458 --> 01:20:29,488
(Adding everything into the kettle)

2057
01:20:30,328 --> 01:20:33,458
(Seasoning it with minced garlic and soy sauce she brought)

2058
01:20:36,628 --> 01:20:38,867
That's a good idea.

2059
01:20:38,868 --> 01:20:41,168
